  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Seasonably warm with a few showers and \nthunderstorms, especially occuring Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure that has granted our region with several days \nof fine summer weather has shifted eastward to New England, with a \nsoutherly return flow now found over our region. This flow will \nbegin to bring mid level moisture back into our region, favoring the \nsouthwestern portion of our region today. Hi-rez models try to \ndevelop a shower over the now warmer Lake Erie waters this morning, \nlifting any showery convection northward towards Buffalo. Given the \ndry airmass this activity will be entering, will just place a low \nchance to slight chance for a shower through midday for areas near \nLake Erie. \n\nAs peak heating increases through the day and instability begins to \nbuild a few additional showers and thunderstorms will be possible \nalong a Lake Erie lake breeze circulation for the hills east of Lake \nErie. Poor mid level lapse rates of around 5 C/km and MUCAPE less \nthan a 1K J/KG will likely lead to any thunderstorms being just \ngarden variety. \n\nMoisture pooling ahead of a shortwave trough tonight will build mid \nlevel moisture further for the day Sunday. Though the first \nshortwave will pass in the early morning hours of Sunday, a \nsecondary shortwave trough will add additional lift for Sunday \nafternoon, that with the presence of deeper instability and \nincreased spatial coverage of this instability we should see \nscattered showers and thunderstorms developing across WNY during the \nafternoon hours. Though this activity will likely develop higher \ncores owing to increase mid level lapse rates, the lack of much wind \nflow aloft will again likely keep any thunderstorms below severe \nthresholds. \n\nTemperatures SUnday will be similar to  Saturday, however what will \nbe notable will be the increase in humidity, with dewpoints Sunday \nafternoon rising into the mid 60s. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather will return Monday and Tuesday \nwith increasing coverage of showers and thunderstorms. \n\nSeveral additional shortwave troughs will dive southeastward over a \nburgeon ridge of high pressure over the Rockie Mountains next Monday \nand Tuesday. This pattern will allow for a long wave trough over \nHudson Bay to expand southward, with its associated cooler airmass \naloft settling southward. \n\nA more humid airmass to start the week will fuel instability to \nbecome greater such that more widespread showers and thunderstorms \nwill become likely. This activity will likely become taller with mid \nlevel lapse rates increasing to over 7 C/km, however the faster flow \nand longer 0-6 km bulk shear vectors are found just to our west and \nsouth over the Ohio Valley. Cannot rule out a few stronger storms \nMonday and Tuesday, especially towards SW NYS. Additional forcing \nahead of a cold front Monday night and into Tuesday could make this \nan active period. \n\nKey Message 3...Upper level trough to bring chances for convection \nalong with a cooler airmass Wednesday and through the end of the \nweek. \n\nFollowing a cold front passage Tuesday, an upper level trough of low \npressure will settle over the eastern Great Lakes. Dewpoints will be \nlower, making for a more comfortable end to the work week...even \nthrough air temperatures remain in the 70s and lower 80s. Steepening \nlapse rates with the associated cooler airmass aloft will lend to \nisolated to scattered afternoon convection blossoming. There are \nmodel differences on how fast this upper level trough and moisture \nexits our region, and while much of the time will be rainfree, there \nwill be chances, especially towards the eastern zones for showers \nand thunderstorm through the remainder of the work week within the \npresence of this upper level trough.",
